<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Jacopo I da Carrara</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. <a href="/years/1324">1324</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/14th-century">14th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Jacopo I da <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carrara">Carrara</a> was a Paduan nobleman elected lord of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/padua">Padua</a> in 1318, when the commune sought a strong leader against Cangrande della Scala of Verona. His election founded the rule of the Carrara family, which lasted with interruptions until 1405. He died in 1324, and Padua fell to Verona four years later.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Jacopo, also called Giacomo and known as the Great (Grande), was a Guelph who led the Paduans to war against Verona in 1311 over the disputed possession of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vicenza">Vicenza</a>. Shortly after 25 July 1318, faced with the threat of the Ghibellines and continuous feuding inside the city, the Paduan aristocracy elected him defensor, protector and gubernator in perpetuity. He is usually considered the first signore of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/padua">Padua</a>, and his election marks the city's passage from commune to the rule of a single lord, a regime its contemporaries called a signoria.</p><p>His election owed something to an alliance between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/padua">Padua</a>'s own Ghibelline and Guelph factions, and after it many Ghibelline exiles returned. He governed with the advice of the leading citizens, and his rule was marked by unity within the city. That same year he sent the poet <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/albertino-mussato">Albertino Mussato</a>, who objected to the signoria and longed for the old commune, into exile.</p><p>In 1319 the Ghibelline Cangrande I della Scala besieged <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/padua">Padua</a> and demanded Jacopo's abdication as the price of peace. Jacopo stepped down for a time to save the city, and the signoria was transferred to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frederick-the-fair">Frederick the Fair</a>, a contender for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holy-roman-empire">Holy Roman Empire</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Jacopo married Anna, daughter of Pietro Gradenigo, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/doge-of-venice">Doge of Venice</a>. Anna bore him one daughter, Taddea, and died in 1321. Jacopo died in 1324 and was buried in the Church of the Eremitani in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/padua">Padua</a>. On her father's death Taddea moved to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a>, where she married Cangrande's nephew <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mastino-ii-della-scala">Mastino II della Scala</a> in the Church of San Giorgio Maggiore. The Carraresi dynasty he founded ruled Padua until 1405.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Jacopo I da Carrara” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Pressure from Cangrande della Scala of Verona led the citizens in 1318 to make <b>Jacopo I da Carrara</b> their lord, beginning the Lordship of Padua.</blockquote><a href="/state/commune-of-padua">Commune of Padua</a></li><li><blockquote>Threatened by Cangrande della Scala, the Commune of Padua in 1318 elected <b>Jacopo I da Carrara</b> as its lord.</blockquote><a href="/state/lordship-of-padua">Lordship of Padua</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/padua">Padua</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/padua">Padua</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=45.41,11.87&amp;year=1314" aria-label="Padua on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Jacopo I da Carrara"><image href="/maps/globes/g000n60.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5708 6171h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5708 6171h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=45.41,11.87&amp;year=1314">What was here in 1314? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
